xt": "{\"input\":\"You are implementing a SPECIFIC NEXT ACTION starting from the specification.\\n\\nYou are implementing a SPECIFIC NEXT ACTION on top of a known-good HIP C++ baseline for MI300X.\\n\\nOriginal Specification:\\nName: gemm_bf16_var_mnk\\nType: gemm\\n\\nAxes:\\n  M: variable (rows of A and output)\\n  N: variable (rows of B (before transpose) and cols of output)\\n  K: variable (reduction dimension)\\n\\nInputs:\\n  A: [M, K] (DType.BFLOAT16) - lhs matrix\\n  B: [N, K] (DType.BFLOAT16) - rhs matrix, transposed in matmul\\n\\nOutputs:\\n  C: [M, N] (DType.BFLOAT16) - gemm output\\n\\n\\nReference Implementation:\\nimport torch\\n\\ndef run(A, B):\\n    C = torch.matmul(A, B.T)\\n    return C\\n\\nKnown-Good Base Implementation (start from this; do not include any other previous code):\\n(no base code; start from spec)\\n\\nChosen Next Action (apply this action):\\nWorld Model: Chosen Next Action (from decision tree node)\\n- node_id: n1_balanced_dense\\n- base_node_id: root\\n- title: Prototype balanced dense GEMM path\\n- difficulty_1_to_5: 3\\n- description: Implement a single dense GEMM family that computes full-K accumulation per output tile and targets the common moderate/large M,N regime.\\n- expected_vs_baseline_factor: 1.45x\\n- rationale: Most workloads are often dominated by balanced shapes; this path sets a strong baseline with minimal algorithmic complexity.\\n\\nRules:\\n- Implement ONLY the chosen action; keep everything else as close as possible to the base implementation.\\n- Keep changes small and single-iteration implementable.\\n- Preserve correctness and the same PyTorch binding / entry points.\\n- Return only the full updated XML blocks (no explanations, no markdown).\\n\\nIMPORTANT: Generate code in XML format with exactly 3 files with these strict names:\\n\\n<header_file name=\\\"kernel.h\\\">\\n- All HIP kernel function declarations\\n- Host function declarations\\n- Any necessary struct/type definitions\\n- Include guards and necessary headers\\n</header_file>\\n\\n<cuda_file name=\\\"kernel.cu\\\">\\n- All __global__ kernel implementations\\n- All __device__ helper functions\\n- HIP-specific optimizations and memory patterns\\n- Proper error checking and memory management\\n</cuda_file>\\n\\n<cpp_file name=\\\"main.cpp\\\">\\n- Host function that launches kernels\\n- Memory allocation and data transfer management\\n- Device management and error handling\\n- Entry point function named \\\"run\\\" that can be called to execute the implementation\\n- Handle both args and kwargs properly\\n- Move CPU data to GPU, execute kernels, and return results to CPU\\n- MUST include PyTorch C++ extension bindings using PYBIND11_MODULE\\n- The \\\"run\\\" function must be exposed to Python through the binding\\n- Include proper tensor type conversion between PyTorch tensors and device pointers\\n- Include all necessary PyTorch headers: #include <torch/extension.h>\\n</cpp_file>\\n\\nCode Generation Guidelines:\\n- Use modern HIP features appropriate for MI300X\\n- Optimize memory coalescing and reduce bank conflicts\\n- Utilize shared memory effectively for data reuse\\n- Consider occupancy and register usage\\n- Implement proper error checking with hipGetLastError()\\n- Use appropriate grid and block dimensions for the problem size\\n- Leverage constant memory for frequently accessed read-only data\\n- Use PyTorch tensor API (torch::Tensor) for all tensor arguments in the \\\"run\\\" function\\n- Convert PyTorch tensors to HIP pointers using .data_ptr<float>() or similar methods\\n- Ensure proper HIP stream synchronization and error handling\\n\\n** You MUST use MFMA to utilize the matrix cores on MI300X! ** For each round, you can see your current best solution and the previous round's summary, therefore you can implement the kernel step by step. ROCm/HIP compatibility note (when targeting AMD/MI300X): do not include <ATen/BFloat16.h> (prefer <c10/util/BFloat16.h> or only <torch/extension.h>), avoid